------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Background

A Verkada air-quality sensor carries a temperature probe on a slow I2C bus,
reachable only through a vendor blob:

    int tempdev_read(float *celsius);

    returns      0   *celsius holds a fresh reading
                -1   the device NACKed the transfer; *celsius untouched

    blocking     ~600 ms on the real part: one long I2C conversion. A NACK is
                 only discovered when the transaction times out, so a FAILING
                 read blocks just as long as a good one.
                 This harness scales that down to 60 ms so the whole suite
                 finishes in a couple of seconds (and to 250 ms for the two
                 coalescing phases, to make them about coalescing rather than
                 about thread-start jitter). Nothing in your code may depend
                 on the number.

    re-entrancy  STRICTLY ONE CALLER AT A TIME. Two threads inside
                 tempdev_read() at the same moment interleave their I2C
                 transactions and corrupt the bus. There is no internal lock.
                 The fake probe in main.c detects concurrent entry and fails
                 the run — that is the whole point of the exercise.

Several unrelated subsystems want the temperature: the fan controller wants it
every few seconds, the MQTT uplink wants it whenever it publishes, a local HTTP
handler wants it per request, and the web UI polls a history graph. They all
run on their own threads and none of them may be made to wait 600 ms because
somebody else already is.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Tasks

Part 1.
  Implement an on-demand cached reader:

      int temp_cache_init(void);
      void temp_cache_deinit(void);
      int temp_get(float *out, uint64_t max_age_us);

  - If the cached reading is fresher than max_age_us (strictly: age <
    max_age_us) return it immediately, without touching the bus.
  - Otherwise refresh. If N threads ask at the same time and all of them need
    a refresh, EXACTLY ONE tempdev_read() may happen. The other N-1 wait for
    that result; they must not queue N reads, and they must not be serialised
    behind each other.
  - max_age_us == 0 therefore means "force one refresh and give me its
    result", never "read the device N times".
  - The refresh can fail. Waiters must not spin or hang. Decide what a caller
    gets when the refresh NACKed — the last good value flagged as stale, or an
    error — and document the choice. Do not let a failed refresh turn N
    waiters into N more vendor reads.
  - Return codes are in temp_cache.h: TEMP_OK / TEMP_STALE / TEMP_NODATA.
  - Report counters through temp_cache_stats() so the harness can prove the
    coalescing actually happened.

  There is no background sampler thread in this problem. Nothing reads the
  probe except a caller who wants a value.

Part 2.
  Implement range statistics over the last hour:

      int temp_range_stats(uint64_t t0, uint64_t t1,
                           float *min, float *max, float *avg);
      int temp_history_add(uint64_t t_us, float celsius);

  min / max / mean of every recorded sample with t0 <= ts <= t1 (both ends
  inclusive), over whatever the 1-hour window still holds. Returns 0, or -1
  when t0 > t1 or the range holds no sample.

  temp_get()'s refresh path records each successful reading itself; the
  harness also calls temp_history_add() directly to load an hour of history
  without waiting an hour.

  The cost of a query must be bounded by the WINDOW, not by the number of
  samples inside it. Reference answer: TEMP_NBUCKETS (60) fixed buckets of
  TEMP_BUCKET_US (1 minute), each carrying min/max/sum/count, so at most 60
  O(1) folds per query — plus the raw samples, so the PARTIAL bucket at each
  end of the range is still exact. Write down, in comments, how this compares
  with (a) scanning every sample and (b) a sparse table / segment tree, and
  when each of those is the right call.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Expected behaviour (what main.c turns into PASS/FAIL)

  - init touches no bus at all; a cold cache on a wedged bus -> TEMP_NODATA
    after exactly one attempted read.
  - 8 threads released simultaneously onto a stale cache -> exactly ONE
    tempdev_read(), all 8 get TEMP_OK with the SAME value, stats show 1 leader
    and 7 coalesced.
  - The same wave on a wedged bus -> still exactly one read, all 8 get
    TEMP_STALE with the previous good value, nobody hangs; the next wave after
    recovery gets one read and a new value.
  - A fresh cache is served with zero bus traffic; 2000 cache hits cost far
    less than one vendor read (worst case measured, must be < read/4).
  - The fake probe fails the run if two threads are ever inside it at once.
  - Range stats are compared against a brute-force scan of ground truth the
    harness injected: the whole window, 500 random ranges, and the boundaries
    (empty history, t0 > t1, entirely in the future, inside the window but
    before the first sample, t0 == t1, a range that only partly covers the
    newest bucket, exactly one bucket, a sub-bucket range, samples older than
    the window). Nothing is hard-coded; the PRNG is a fixed-seed xorshift, not
    rand().
  - A full-window query must inspect far fewer raw samples than the window
    holds (stats.last_scanned), i.e. a brute-force scan does not pass.
  - 4 threads hammering both APIs for 1.2 s: no value the probe never
    produced, every range answer still exact, worst temp_get within one vendor
    read plus slack, worst range query under read/4, and the error path kept
    warm by a NACK every 7th call.
  - deinit called while a read is in flight waits for the bus, releases the
    caller, is idempotent, and temp_get() afterwards returns TEMP_NODATA.

  Last line is exactly "ALL CHECKS PASSED (0 failed)" or "N CHECK(S) FAILED".

