Things the interviewer is listening for   (answer these on paper first)

  1. Eight threads want a value and the cache is stale. Why is "each one calls
     tempdev_read()" wrong on two separate counts, and which of the two does a
     plain mutex around the vendor call fix — and which does it not?
  2. Walk the leader/waiter handshake. What exactly does a waiter wait ON?
     Why is a bare boolean ("a read is in flight") not enough to wake up on,
     and what does the generation counter buy you? What about spurious wakeups?
  3. Why must the mutex be released across tempdev_read()? Name the two things
     that break if you hold it: what happens to a caller who would have been a
     cache HIT, and what happens to the other seven callers?
  4. The refresh NACKs. What do the seven waiters get? If a waiter instead
     "retried by becoming the new leader", what does a wedged bus do to your
     I2C traffic? Where does the retry policy belong?
  5. Could you do Part 1 with an atomic value and no condvar? What is the
     thing an atomic cannot express here? (Hint: sleeping until an event, and
     naming which event.) When would sem_t or a per-call future be better?
  6. Part 2: how much memory is one hour? Give the arithmetic for both the
     aggregates and the raw samples, and say what the worst sample rate is
     when the device is only read on demand at 60 ms per read.
  7. Part 2: a range query's two ends land in the middle of a bucket. How do
     you answer those exactly, and what is the worst-case work in terms of
     buckets and samples? Why is that bounded by the window and not by n?
  8. Part 2: compare buckets with (a) scanning all samples and (b) a sparse
     table or segment tree. What does each fix, what does each cost, and which
     would you pick if the query were "min over an arbitrary range of the last
     30 days"?
  9. gettimeofday is not monotonic. What breaks in the cache freshness test,
     and what breaks in the bucket index, when the clock steps backwards?
     What does "age" even mean then?
 10. Shutdown: deinit is called while a leader is inside a 600 ms bus
     transaction and seven waiters are parked. What must deinit do about each
     of them, in what order, and why can it not just free the state and
     return? What does a caller arriving after deinit get?
